          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes is a condition where the body has difficulty regulating blood sugar levels due to insulin resistance. This means that the cells do not respond properly to insulin, and the pancreas may struggle to produce enough insulin to overcome this resistance. High blood sugar levels can persist as a result. Unlike type 1 diabetes, where the body produces little or no insulin due to an autoimmune response, type 2 diabetes typically develops gradually and may not present symptoms initially. It is important to address prediabetes, which is a stage where blood sugar levels are elevated but not high enough to be classified as diabetes, as early lifestyle changes can have significant benefits (source: General clinical reference).\n\nFor lifestyle guidance regarding type 2 diabetes, some key points include:\n\n1. **Diet**: Focus on a balanced diet that is rich in whole foods, including fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and lean proteins. Reducing sugar and high carbohydrate foods can help manage blood sugar levels. It’s important to be aware that \"sugar-free\" does not mean \"carbohydrate-free,\" as many foods containing starch can still affect blood sugar.\n\n2. **Weight Management**: Achieving and maintaining a healthy weight can improve blood sugar control. Even a small weight loss of around 5% can offer health benefits.\n\n3. **Physical Activity**: Engaging in regular physical activity can help lower blood sugar levels and improve insulin sensitivity. The type and intensity of exercise should be discussed with a clinician, especially if there are any additional health concerns.\n\n4. **Monitoring and Education**: Self-management is crucial in diabetes care. Regular monitoring of blood sugar levels, adherence to advice from healthcare providers, and ongoing education about diabetes management can significantly enhance health outcomes (sources: National diabetes association nutrition guidance and physical activity guidance).\n\nThese lifestyle changes can work as part of a comprehensive management plan. Always consult with your healthcare provider for personalized advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
